  "body": "Follow-up to #793 (built-in taper holder library).\n\nThe tool editor can now drop a built-in holder (\"BT30 ER20\", \"BT40 Weldon 12 mm\", \"CAT40 ER32\", ...) into a milling tool's holder through one undoable command, `SetToolBody::library(world, tool, entry, gauge_len, projection)`, built on `SetToolBody::spun`. No MCP tool sets a tool's holder or body today, so an agent can't do it headlessly.\n\n**What:** an MCP tool `set_tool_holder` (and a script verb where it fits) in `crates/app/src/mcp/`:\n- `tool` (required);\n- either `library` (an entry name, as `camcraft_tools::holder_library::HolderEntry::name` gives it), with optional `gauge_len` and `projection` in mm,\n- or `points` (a drawn spun profile, `[{z, r, arc}]`),\n- or `clear: true` (back to the holder cylinder).\n\nIt goes through the same `SetToolBody` command, so it's one undo step. A `list_holder_library` tool (or a field on `list_tool_catalog`) returns the entry names and their dimensions.\n\n**Done means:**\n- An MCP test sets \"BT30 ER20\" on a tool and `get_job` shows the holder.\n- `undo` restores the previous holder.\n- An unknown entry or a shank the holder can't take is refused by name.\n- The camcraft-mcp skill's tool table lists the new tool.\n\nPart of #743.\n",
